<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilkhan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilkhan was the title adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> and his successors, meaning a subordinate khan. It acknowledged the nominal supremacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The title gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the Mongol state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1307 Bularghu, the Mongol representative in Cilicia and a recent convert to Islam, murdered King Levon III, the former king Het'um II and their party; Oshin drove him out and the <b>Ilkhan</b> Oljeitu executed him, but the kingdom had lost its ally.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hulagu made Maragha in Azerbaijan his capital, later supplanted by Tabriz, and adopted the title “<b>Ilkhan</b>”, usually understood as a subordinate khan acknowledging the supremacy of the Great Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Real power lay with ministers such as the Pervane, whose ambiguous dealings with the Mamluks ended in disaster: in 1277 the Mamluk sultan Baybars invaded and defeated a Mongol-Seljuk force at Elbistan, after which the <b>Ilkhan</b> Abaqa executed the Pervane and tightened direct Mongol rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1290 the <b>Ilkhan</b> Arghun told Pope Nicholas IV that whoever prays to Eternal Tengri with righteous thoughts has done as much as if he had received baptism.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of the last strong <b>Ilkhan</b>, Abu Sa'id, in 1335, he governed central Anatolia on his own account, first in the name of rival Mongol princes and from 1341 as sultan, minting coins in his own name.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretnids">Eretnids</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Ilkhan</b> Abu Sa'id died in 1335, his family made itself master of Shiraz and the province.</blockquote><a href="/state/injuid-dynasty">Injuid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
